cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this artifact, please cite the paper (v2)."
title: >-
  Whether a Sensitivity-Label Effect Can Be Measured at an MCP-to-A2A
  Handoff Depends on the Task Framing: A Pre-Registered Sweep
authors:
  - family-names: Mahapatra
    given-names: Arpan Kumar
    email: arpan.arpan.mohapatra@gmail.com
abstract: >-
  A three-phase, pre-registered study of whether an explicit sensitivity
  label on a locally-read record changes verbatim field egress at a Model
  Context Protocol (MCP) to Agent2Agent (A2A) handoff, scored by an
  exact-substring, judge-free detector. Phase 6 (two arms, 640 trials)
  found a large confidential-vs-public contrast but could not attribute
  it to either label. Phase 7 added an unlabeled baseline (480 trials);
  three of four models floored on both the confidential and unlabeled
  arms, leaving that contrast unmeasured. Phase 8 was a two-round,
  six-framing task-wording sweep with a stopping rule fixed in advance,
  seeking a framing whose unlabeled-arm rate lands in [0.25, 0.70] for at
  least three of four models; no framing met the rule, the stopping rule
  fired, and the ~13,184-trial main study was not executed. At n = 12,
  framing F3 remains unresolved under interval analysis. A public-arm
  contrast collected outside the frozen analysis plan is reported as
  exploratory. Whether a label's effect can be measured here depends on
  the task framing; the study does not establish that task-framing
  effects are universally larger than label effects.
version: "2.0"
